WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  on bangladesh's south coast  homes don't just flood they disappear  we're heading to a village called  calabogie  surrounded by a rising river battered by  cyclones this land of mud and sediment  is being washed away taking homes  and hope along with it  rising sea levels in the bay of bengal  downstream  mean river tides here are getting higher  storm surges are more intense and  dangerous  than  people here are getting desperate there  is no land left for farming  fishing is all they've got and to try  and escape  poverty and debt they are prepared to  break the law  this village is on the edge of the  protected sunderbans forest  it's a unesco world heritage site and  it's illegal to fish here  but it's a rich habitat for wildlife  [Applause]  and at night next to the forest edge we  see flashlights on the water  there are dozens of fishermen here  when we get to nazarel's shake he thinks  we're the coast guard coming to chase  him away  these fine mesh nets are illegal too  but they catch more what he's doing now  is he's drawn the nets in and he  is sorting what has come up  and what he's looking for are these tiny  shrimp fries  like shrimp larvae and they look like  very very fine hairs and he's been out  here about  six hours and he's told us he's probably  caught enough to make about  13 dollars  tonight he says the catch is good it's a  brief reprieve  [Applause]  because although no one wants to leave  here in reality  they're running out of places to go  over there about 500 meters away is a  small island with homes on it until  about a year ago  that was connected to this bit of land  and it wasn't just  a road to walk on it was a stretch of  land that had homes on it where people  lived and when you see this and you  think about climate change goals and  climate change targets you like to think  that  action can make a difference but really  for people living here it's already too  late  this is why bangladesh says the world  must act  with this year's climate conference  needed not to talk about what will  happen  but about what's happening right here  right now  catherine of autopsy sky news in  kalibogi bangladesh

